Task 4: Contextual embedding similarity
Research question: Can posts that are used in contexts similar to known dog whistles help us find other candidates?
Method:
  Seed terms:
    -Took the 5 most frequent known terms in the dog_whistle column (at least 20 posts each): based, beta, blue, gc, single.
  Embeddings:
    -Embedded all 5,000 posts with all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (sentence-transformers).
    -Averaged the embeddings of each seed term's posts and compared them with all posts that do not contain the term (cosine similarity).
    -Kept the top 150 posts per seed term (750 rows).
  Candidate selection:
    -Took 1-3 word phrases found in at least 5 of the similar posts, excluding stopword-only phrases and exact glossary terms.
    -Ranked them by enrichment (frequency in similar posts relative to the whole dataset).
    -Checked against the glossary (yes / variant / no).
Results:
  -878 candidates: 865 not in the glossary, 13 variants of glossary terms, 0 exact matches (removed by design).
  -Similarity scores 0.44 to 0.71. Similar posts come mainly from gender-critical/trans and red-pill communities.
  -Plausible in-group vocabulary: alpha, chads, white knights, red pill / red pilled, beta males, beta bux.
  -Noise:
    Ordinary phrases: life of, partners, successful, straight up, can't even.
  -Artifacts of the glossary:
    6 of the 13 variants are false matches from common words: based on, a single, every single, the blue, my blue.
  -Limitations: similar posts share a topic, not necessarily coded meaning; some seed words are ordinary words; no accuracy score because there is no verified list of new dog whistles.
  -Related work: FETCH! (Sasse et al., ACL 2025) uses the same embedding model; see References.md.
